th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/kernel.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/kernel.html","questions":[{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the kernel of a linear map $A\\colon V\\to W$?","options":["The set of vectors $v\\in V$ such that $Av=0$.","The set of all vectors in $W$ not reached by $A$.","The set of all outputs of $A$.","The set of scalars used by $V$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c47c29292a791d17d7cc402a","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What kind of subset is the kernel of a linear map?","options":["A basis of the range.","A subspace of the domain.","A scalar field.","A subspace of the codomain only."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e56cab43bcfdcf53fdaaf7cf","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A(x,y)=(x,0)$, which vectors are in $\\ker A$?","options":["Only $(1,0)$.","All $(0,y)$ with $y\\in\\mathbb{R}$.","All $(x,y)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","All $(x,0)$ with $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2b205d520b8472883e27145b","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the kernel useful for studying injectivity?","options":["The kernel is always empty for injective maps.","A map is injective exactly when its kernel is the whole domain.","A linear map is injective exactly when its kernel is $\\{0\\}$.","Injectivity ignores what happens to zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"66f64a5e32862f46f5a77c9c","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can the kernel be empty?","options":["Yes, if the codomain is larger than the domain.","Yes, if the map is injective, as no vector is sent to zero.","No, it always contains the zero vector of the domain.","No, because it always equals the whole domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"52c95a76dd534afec47bf9c7","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_005_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\boldsymbol{A}(x,y)=(x-y,\\,2x-2y)$, a student checks $\\boldsymbol{A}(0,0)=(0,0)$ and concludes $\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}=\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$. Which assessment is correct?","options":["Wrong: $\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}$ is the whole line $\\{(t,t)\\colon t\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}$.","Right: $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{0}=\\boldsymbol{0}$ is exactly what $\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}=\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$ requires.","Wrong: $\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}=\\{(0,0),(1,1)\\}$, the two solutions.","Wrong: $\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}=\\mathbb{R}^2$, as the two outputs are multiples of each other."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1b1938427abd3bea99380d7d","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_009_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the kernel of the zero map $\\boldsymbol{Z}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\boldsymbol{Z}(\\boldsymbol{v})=\\boldsymbol{0}$?","options":["$\\mathbb{R}^2$","$\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{R}^2\\setminus\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$","$\\{\\boldsymbol{0}\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"56e9bffc9e298923091da1b3","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_019_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $A(x,y,z)=x-y$, what is one parametrisation of $\\ker A$?","options":["$\\{(s,t,s+t)\\colon s,t\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}$","$\\{(s,-s,t)\\colon s,t\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}$","$\\{(s,s,t)\\colon s,t\\in\\mathbb{R}\\}$","$\\{(0,0,0)\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0050bc26fea4e6ff464992ce","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_hq_b3_kernel_020_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}\\colon V\\to W$ be linear and suppose $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{u}=\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{v}$ for some $\\boldsymbol{u},\\boldsymbol{v}\\in V$. Which statement must hold?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{u}-\\boldsymbol{v}\\in\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}=\\boldsymbol{v}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}+\\boldsymbol{v}\\in\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}$","$\\boldsymbol{u}\\in\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}\\in\\ker\\boldsymbol{A}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f210b6f45e6e63ed506a5fae","time_limit":30}]}}